<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Water</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The water kernel: one definition of where the surface is. A body is a spline and a
            profile, a sea state is a spectrum summed as Gerstner waves, and a zone rasterises both
            into a field of surface height, flow and the ground beneath. The evaluator over that
            field is what the vertex stage, the buoyancy solver and a gameplay query all call — so a
            boat floats at the height the water is drawn at. Pure arithmetic over arrays and structs,
            with no device and no world, which is also what a dedicated server runs.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>water;ocean;river;buoyancy;gerstner;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            One reference, for Vixen.Terrain's reason (docs/plan/35 § D1): the kernel is what the
            dedicated server runs. A headless build has no device and still has to answer how deep
            the water is at a position for every swimming character and every boat it simulates, so
            nothing here may open one. The physics join is a separate assembly rather than a
            reference from here to Jolt.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Mathematics\Vixen.Core.Mathematics.csproj" />

        <!--
            The quadtree, and only the quadtree — docs/plan/35 § D4: "one quadtree, not two". Unreal
            has a landscape LOD system and a water LOD system, with two morphs, two sets of bias cvars
            and two ways to get a crack; PatchSelector is the descent both consumers share, and the
            no-crack and morph-continuity tests are written once because of it. Vixen.Terrain opens no
            device either, so this costs the kernel nothing it was promising not to have.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Terrain\Vixen.Terrain.csproj" />

        <!-- What the generated descriptors are registered into. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Reflection\Vixen.Core.Reflection.csproj" />

        <!--
            The descriptors the editor's YAML binder reads — a .vxwaves is a document an author
            edits. Analyzers do not flow through a ProjectReference, so each assembly that declares a
            contract runs this itself.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

        <!--
            And the binary ones, on Vixen.Foliage's terms. A .vxwaves ships as its serialized
            WaterWavesAsset rather than as text — docs/plan/35 § D6's one asset kind has a runtime
            reader, `AssetWaterSource` — and a type no serializer is registered for cannot be written
            by the importer or read by a game, on a trimmed build or otherwise.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator\Vixen.Core.Serialization.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Water.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
